final_entries.prompt:
My async function is returning [object Promise] instead of the actual data. Here is my code:

async function fetchUser(id) {
  return fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
}

const user = fetchUser(123);
console.log(user.name); // undefined

final_entries.response:
The issue is that fetch() returns a Response object, not the parsed JSON. You need to call .json() on the response and await it. Fix it like this:

async function fetchUser(id) {
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  return response.json(); // Parse the JSON body
}

const user = await fetchUser(123); // Don't forget to await here too
console.log(user.name);

Two key mistakes: (1) fetch() gives you a Response, not data — you must call .json() or .text(), and (2) you weren't awaiting the fetchUser call, so you were getting a Promise instead of the resolved value.

final_entries.prompt:
My C++ program crashes with a segmentation fault when I try to access elements of a dynamically allocated array. Here's the relevant code:

int* createArray(int size) {
    int arr[size];
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) arr[i] = i * 2;
    return arr;
}

int main() {
    int* data = createArray(10);
    cout << data[0] << endl;
    return 0;
}

final_entries.response:
The problem is that createArray returns a pointer to a local variable. The array arr is allocated on the stack inside createArray, and when the function returns, that memory is freed. Accessing it afterward is undefined behavior (usually a segfault).

Fix by allocating on the heap instead:

int* createArray(int size) {
    int* arr = new int[size];  // Heap allocation
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) arr[i] = i * 2;
    return arr;
}

// And don't forget to delete[] data in main!

Better yet, use std::vector<int> to avoid manual memory management entirely.

final_entries.prompt:
My Java program hangs indefinitely when iterating over a HashMap. It seems to work fine with small data but freezes with larger datasets. The key class is:

public class Person {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return name.hashCode();
    }
    // No equals() override
}

final_entries.response:
The hang occurs because your hashCode() only uses name, but you've overridden hashCode without overriding equals(). By Java's contract, if two objects are equal by equals(), they must have the same hashCode — but the reverse isn't required.

However, the real issue is likely that multiple people with the same name are being placed in the same bucket, and without a proper equals() method, HashMap gets confused looking up keys, potentially causing infinite loops during collision resolution.

Fix by adding equals():

@Override
public boolean equals(Object obj) {
    if (this == obj) return true;
    if (!(obj instanceof Person)) return false;
    Person other = (Person) obj;
    return name.equals(other.name) && age == other.age;
}

Also consider using Objects.hash(name, age) for hashCode.

final_entries.prompt:
I'm trying to center a div both horizontally and vertically within its parent using flexbox, but it's only centering horizontally:

.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    height: 100vh;
}

.child {
    width: 200px;
    height: 200px;
    background: blue;
}

final_entries.response:
You're missing the align-items property. Here's what's happening:

- justify-content: center centers items along the main axis (horizontal by default)
- To center on the cross axis (vertical), you need align-items: center

Updated CSS:

.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;  /* horizontal centering */
    align-items: center;      /* vertical centering - add this! */
    height: 100vh;
}

Alternatively, you can use the shorthand:

.container {
    display: flex;
    place-items: center;      /* centers both axes */
    height: 100vh;
}

final_entries.prompt:
I'm getting a borrow checker error in Rust that I don't understand:

fn process(data: &mut Vec<String>) {
    let item = data[0].clone();
    data.push(item);
    println!("{}", data[0]);
}

error[E0502]: cannot borrow `data` as immutable because it is also borrowed as mutable

final_entries.response:
The error occurs because Rust is being conservative about aliasing. Even though you're only reading data[0] after pushing, the compiler sees that data is mutably borrowed for the push() call, and then you try to immutably borrow it again for the println!

The issue is that data.push() could potentially reallocate the vector's internal buffer, invalidating any previous references. Rust prevents this potential problem.

Solutions:

1. Clone the value you need before mutating:
   let first = data[0].clone();
   data.push(item);
   println!("{}", first);

2. Or use indices separately:
   data.push(item);
   println!("{}", data[0]);

Both approaches work because you're not holding a reference while mutating.
